### 4. Code Splitting vs. Lazy Loading

While "Code Splitting" and "Lazy Loading" are frequently used together, they refer to **two distinct sides of the same optimization strategy**:

| Concept            | **Code Splitting**                                                                                      | **Lazy Loading**                                                                                  |
| ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **What is it?**    | The **architectural technique** of breaking a monolithic JavaScript bundle into smaller files (chunks). | The **execution pattern** of delaying the fetch/rendering of a chunk until it is actually needed. |
| **Focus**          | **Build-time** asset organization (Bundler level - Webpack, Vite, Rollup).                              | **Runtime** execution strategy (Application level - React, Browser).                              |
| **Analogy**        | Slicing a large book into individual, separate chapters.                                                | Opening and reading Chapter 5 only when you reach Chapter 5.                                      |
| **Code Mechanism** | Dynamic imports (`import('./Component')`) creating separate `.js` chunk files.                          | Using `React.lazy()`, `<Suspense>`, or intersection observers to load assets on user demand.      |

#### How they work together

* **Code Splitting** creates the separate `admin-panel.chunk.js` file at build time.
* **Lazy Loading** ensures `admin-panel.chunk.js` is only fetched over the network when the user actually clicks the "Open Admin" tab at runtime.
